.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report bugs to the maintainers of the RECORD workflow tools\.
.SH "BUGS"
The \fB\-\-assignment\-operator\fR and \fB\-\-argument\-types\-library\fR option parsers call \fBsplit(a, "=", limit=2)\fR unconditionally before checking whether \fB=\fR is present, then reference the undefined local variable \fBval\fR (instead of \fBvalue\fR) when testing for \fBnothing\fR\. This will raise an \fBUndefVarError\fR at runtime whenever these options are used\.
.P
The same \fBval\fR vs \fBvalue\fR bug affects the \fB\-a=\fR, \fB\-l=\fR, and \fB\-o=\fR short option handlers\.
.P
The \fB\-\-run\fR / \fB\-R\fR flag is recognised and sets \fBexecute = true\fR in \fBparse_before_double_dash\fR, but the return tuple from that function omits \fBexecute\fR, so \fBmain\fR never receives it and the flag has no effect\.
.P
The \fBparse_before_double_dash\fR return statement when \fB\-\-\fR is not found returns \fBassignment_operator\fR and \fBsuppress\fR in swapped positions compared to the return statement when \fB\-\-\fR is found, meaning the caller will silently assign the wrong values to these variables when \fB\-\-\fR is absent\.
.P
In the passthrough parser, the \fB>\fR (stdout redirect) branch contains a syntax error: \fBname =\fR appears on a line by itself with no right\-hand side, which will cause a parse error at load time\.
.P
In several branches the local variable \fBassingment\fR (a typo of \fBassignment\fR) is assigned rather than \fBassignment\fR, silently discarding the space assignment operator for these cases\.
.P
The \fBidentifier\fR variable is referenced in the \fBrequired\fR argument output inside \fBpreprocessor\fR but is never assigned a value in \fBmain\fR, so it will always be \fBnothing\fR in the generated output\.
.P
The \fB\-\-suppress\-alternatives\fR option is spelled \fB\-\-suppress\-alterantives\fR (transposed letters) in the help text, which does not match the \fB\-\-suppress\-alternatives\fR spelling used in the parser\.
.P
The library files are opened in append mode (\fB"a"\fR), meaning repeated invocations accumulate duplicate entries rather than overwriting the previous output\.
